## This is a token hand-off, not a redirect

There is no OAuth redirect, no callback route, and no authorization-code exchange
to build. The backend endpoint below takes a Google ID token and returns a
session in one request/response pair. `GoogleLoginButton` already does this end
to end: it obtains a Google ID token through the generated hook, calls
`useGoogleLogin()` with `{ idToken }`, and forwards the response to
`persistSession` from the session seam.

## API contract

| Function | Path | Request | Response | Notes |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `loginWithGoogle` | `{{ googleSsoLoginEndpoint }}` | `{ token: string }` | `{ accessToken: string, tokenType: string, expiresIn: number \| null }` | Establishes the session directly - no challenge token, no `token_type` discriminant to branch on |

## Obtaining the Google ID token

`use-google-identity-services.ts` loads Google's own Identity Services script
from its CDN URL (`https://accounts.google.com/gsi/client`) rather than an npm
package such as `@react-oauth/google` - this package never installs frontend
dependencies. `GoogleLoginButton` renders Google's own button into the hook's
container ref and forwards the resulting credential straight to `useGoogleLogin`.
If you'd rather use `@react-oauth/google` or a different integration, treat both
generated files as a reference implementation and replace them - nothing else
depends on their internals beyond the `{ containerRef, isError }` shape.

## Required environment variable

The frontend needs its own OAuth Client ID env var to initialize the Identity
Services SDK. **This installer already added `VITE_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID` to
`src/config/env.ts`** for you (see "Files written" above) - set its value in
your `.env` to **the same OAuth Client ID** as the backend's `GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID`.
Google issues one Client ID per app, and the ID token the frontend obtains is
only valid against the backend's `Google\Client` if both sides were configured
with the same one. This package doesn't write `.env` files for you (see the
backend TODO output for the same caveat) - add the value yourself and to your
deployment secrets.

## Session storage

`GoogleLoginButton` already calls `persistSession` from the shared session seam
(`@/services/auth/session`) on a successful login - the same seam every other
login method in this package uses. There is nothing left to wire here unless you
change that seam's storage mechanism.

## Error states

`InvalidGoogleTokenException` returns HTTP 400 with error code
`Invalid_google_token` when the ID token fails verification (bad format,
expired, or issued for a different Client ID). Surface this as a generic
"Google sign-in failed, try again" message - the code doesn't distinguish
*why* verification failed, so don't build UI that assumes it can.

## Adding another SSO provider

Every provider's generated frontend files live under
`services/auth/sso/{provider}/` (this file's own files live under
`services/auth/sso/google/`) - each provider is fully isolated, with no shared
client or helper module. This is deliberate: with exactly one real provider
today, a shared `services/auth/sso/shared/*` abstraction would be speculative
generality with no second call site to validate it against. Adding Apple or
Facebook SSO costs, roughly:

- One new `{Provider}SSOFrontendInstaller` (frontend) and 4-5 new frontend
  stubs under `services/auth/sso/{provider}/` - this file's four service stubs
  plus its own TODO doc, mirroring this installer's shape exactly.
- On the backend: one new `{Provider}SSOInstaller`, its own stub directory, and
  reuse (or generalization) of `InvalidGoogleTokenException`'s shape for that
  provider's own invalid-token exception.
- One new route stub, registered through the same `RouteFileRegistrar` this
  driver already uses - no changes needed to the registrar itself.

Nothing about this driver's installer, stubs, or routes needs to change to
support a second provider - if a second provider's `api.ts` ever turns out to
be similar enough to this one to warrant extracting a shared exchange helper,
that's a same-directory refactor to do *then*, once there are two real shapes
to compare, not a decision to make speculatively now.

